-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Interpolation Artifacts:** Standard SVO lacks interpolation and filtering, causing popping artifacts when transitioning between levels and aliasing at distance or close to the viewpoint [S3, unofficial]. This is often mitigated by screen-space blurring or merging LODs via a stitching algorithm to achieve C0-continuity [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Dynamic Updates:** Adding, deleting, or modifying blocks is expensive and may require rebuilding a large part of the octree [S4, unofficial]. The structure assumes static geometry for optimal performance; calculating contours adds overhead to construction speed [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Cache Friendliness:** Octrees are harder to make cache-friendly compared to flat structures [S2, unofficial]. A complete octree of certain depth consumes significant memory, necessitating the sparse representation [S2, unofficial].
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Precision:** SDF values are often stored as 8-bit snorm integers, clamped to [-1, 1]. This limits the precision of the distance field, especially at larger voxel sizes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Artifacts:** Standard SVOs lack interpolation and filtering, causing popping artifacts when transitioning between LoD levels and aliasing at distance or close to the viewpoint [S3, unofficial]. This requires post-processing (screen-space blurring) or stitching algorithms to achieve C0-continuity [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Dynamic Updates:** Adding, deleting, or modifying blocks can be expensive, potentially requiring a large part of the octree to be rebuilt [S4, unofficial]. The structure assumes static geometry for optimal performance; dynamic updates are not efficiently supported on GPU without significant overhead [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Cache Coherence:** Octrees are harder to make cache-friendly compared to flat structures due to pointer chasing [S2, unofficial].
 
